<div class="content-section">

  {% set airhubPrimary = providerA.codes | findCode(providerA.primaryCode) %}
  {% set airhubBest = providerA.codes | bestValidCode %}
  {% set upesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you want the lowest possible per-GB price and like having SMS plus a local number bundled in. Pick <strong>UPeSIM</strong> if you want an established French MVNO with 200+ destinations and don't mind a data-only plan. Both are budget picks sold in fixed data blocks rather than unlimited, so the real decision is about what's bundled in, not which one is "better data."
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: What's Bundled With the Data</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub plans include SMS and, in most destinations, a local phone number alongside the data, which is unusual at this price point. UPeSIM is data-only: no SMS, no local number, calls and texts route through your home carrier's roaming rates instead. If you need to receive a verification text or hand out a number that isn't your own while traveling, Airhub covers that without an add-on; UPeSIM doesn't do it at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    UPeSIM's edge is timing and footprint. Its plans don't start counting down until your phone actually connects to the destination network, so you can install the eSIM at home, well before you fly, with no rush at the airport. Airhub doesn't advertise the same delayed-start mechanic. UPeSIM also covers 200+ countries and territories as a Paris-based MVNO running since 2018, versus Airhub's 190+.
  </p>

  <h2>Price Snapshot (per GB, before any code)</h2>
  <div class="content-box">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Destination</th>
          <th>Airhub</th>
          <th>UPeSIM</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
